  "query_preview": "../Monday through Friday/...The active pattern\ncontinues into next week, with the system impacting the panhandle\nduring the weekend continuing to meander northwestward towards\nPrince William Sound and weaken by Monday morning. Multiple\nshortwaves will push across the Gulf Monday through Tuesday, \nkeeping precipitation and elevated winds in the forecast with each\nwave passing through. \n\nSome uncertainty in the forecast remains for midweek, however, on\nthe synoptic setup and potential for a brief pattern change with\nsnow going into the end of the week, with some model guidance \nsuggesting high pressure building in the Gulf and over the \npanhandle Wednesday alongside a brief cooling and drying trend \nahead of the next system. This solution would bring snow levels \ndown across the northern panhandle and along the coastal range \nWednesday into early Thursday morning, with snow levels down to \n1000 ft or less, and bringing potential for snow at sea level for \nthe northern panhandle Thursday as the next system moves into the \narea. However, there is a lot of uncertainty regarding this, as \nsome guidance suggests warmer air and moisture with the next \nsystem moving in much sooner and not allowing as long of a period \nfor cooling temperatures or lowering snow levels, with a much \nweaker ridge not holding the system back. Other guidance shows \nthis ridge being strong enough to keep precipitation out of the \narea by the end of the week, though at this time this looks to be \nan outlier compared to other solutions. It will be something to \nwatch as confidence increases in a specific solution, however \nthere is a notable probability in seeing some snow or snow mixing \nin with rain at sea levels for Skagway and Haines, potentially \ndown to Icy Strait Corridor, into the end of the week depending on\nhow favorable the setup is for snow.",
